## Key principles for this project

### DRY — Don't Repeat Yourself

If the same code appears in multiple files, that's a problem. For example, the login logic is duplicated in `OplataCart.spec.ts` even though it already lives in `auth.ts`. Always import — never copy-paste.

```typescript
// ❌ Bad — duplicating login logic in every test
const loginField = page.locator('input[name="login"]');
await loginField.fill(process.env.DROPP_EMAIL || '');

// ✅ Good — written once in auth.ts, imported everywhere
import { loginAsUser } from '../auth';
await loginAsUser(page);
```

### Page Object Model (POM)

Locators must not live directly inside tests. If the markup changes, you'll have to update 10 files. A Page Object is a class that describes a page — its elements and the actions you can perform on them.

```typescript
// ❌ Bad — locators scattered throughout the test
const cartIcon = page.locator('div.CartDynamicIcon_container__FrCin');
await cartIcon.click();

// ✅ Good — everything in one place
class CartPage {
  cartIcon = this.page.locator('[data-test-id="cart-icon"]');
  constructor(private page: Page) {}
  async open() { await this.cartIcon.click(); }
}
```

### Never use waitForTimeout

`waitForTimeout` is "let's just hope it loads in time". Playwright has built-in auto-waiting: for elements to appear, for network, for state. `waitForTimeout` makes tests slow and unreliable.

```typescript
// ❌ Bad
await page.waitForTimeout(2000);

// ✅ Good — wait for a specific event
await page.waitForLoadState('networkidle');
// or wait for a specific element
await page.locator('[data-test-id="product-card"]').first().waitFor();
```

### Use data-test-id, not hashed CSS classes

Classes like `.ProductCard_container__ZI_Qx` are auto-generated by CSS Modules and change with every build. Your test will break for no apparent reason.

```typescript
// ❌ Fragile — the hash will change on the next build
page.locator('.ProductCard_container__ZI_Qx')

// ✅ Stable — a selector dedicated to testing
page.locator('[data-test-id="product-card"]')
```

### Assertions (expect) — a test must actually verify something

`console.log('✅ Item added')` is not a check. It's just console output. The test will stay green even if the item was never added. Use `expect`.

```typescript
// ❌ Bad — test is always green no matter what
console.log('✅ Navigated to cart');

// ✅ Good — test fails if something went wrong
await expect(page.locator('[data-test-id="cart-items"]')).toBeVisible();
await expect(page.locator('[data-test-id="checkout-button"]')).toBeEnabled();
```

### baseURL in config

The URL must not be repeated in every test file. Set it once in `playwright.config.ts`.

```typescript
// playwright.config.ts
use: {
  baseURL: 'https://dev.dropp.market',
}

// In the test
await page.goto('/men/catalog/sale'); // short and without duplication
```

